# Facebook Advertising Campaign Analysis

This project analyzes the effectiveness of Facebook advertising and its impact on consumer conversion using real-world data from an anonymous company. The dataset includes ad spend, impressions, clicks, conversions, demographics, and interests.

## Objectives
- Understand how ad spending and demographic targeting influence click-through and purchase rates.
- Determine which age/gender groups respond best to Facebook ads.
- Visualize which variables most strongly contribute to approved conversions.

## Tools Used
- Tableau (visualization)
- Excel (data cleaning)
- PowerPoint (presentation/reporting)

## Files Included
- `facebook_campaign_data.xlsx`
- `Facebookads.twbx`
- `The Influence of User Demographics and Ad Design on Facebook Purchase Behavior.ppt`

## Key Insights
- The highest **conversion rate** was observed in **males aged 30–34, at 6.82%, despite having one of the lower click-through rates (0.012%). This suggests targeted messaging to this group was highly effective at driving purchases.
- Females aged 30–34 had the second-highest conversion rate at 3.83%, with a CTR of 0.016%. While they clicked more than males overall, the conversion efficiency was lower.
- Surprisingly, females aged 45–49, despite having the highest number of clicks (9,441), had the **lowest conversion rate** at 1.19%, indicating that higher engagement does not always translate to more sales.
- Ads with higher spend did not guarantee better performance. For example, the $13,433 spent on females 45–49 generated fewer conversions per dollar compared to the $7,640 spent on males 30–34, who converted at a much higher rate.
- These findings reinforce that demographic targeting, especially by age and gender, plays a more significant role in driving conversions than ad spend alone

